iums and neighborhood retail centers</dd></div></dl></div><div><p class="local-k">Local claim risks</p><ul class="chips"><li>Severe storms</li><li>Wind damage</li><li>Water losses</li><li>Tree damage</li><li>Hail</li></ul></div></div>

<h2>A suburban claim market</h2>
<p>Bear is suburban through and through: subdivisions such as Fox Run, Caravel Farms and Brennan Estates, townhome communities and shopping centers along Route 40. Many homes date from the 1980s and 1990s and are now on their second or third roof, original windows and aging plumbing — all common sources of disputes when a storm or leak hits.</p>
<h2>Townhomes and associations</h2>
<p>Townhome and condo communities in and around Bear raise the question of who insures what: the association’s master policy, the owner’s HO-6 or both. We read the governing documents and then value only the portion your policy is responsible for. For associations, we value common elements and building systems for the board.</p>
<h2>Commercial along the corridors</h2>
<p>Retail centers and small office buildings along Route 40 and Route 7 have low-slope roofs, rooftop HVAC and storefront glass. Insurance appraisal in Bear frequently includes these commercial properties, where the details of membrane roofing and code upgrades drive the numbers.</p>
<h2>What we see most</h2>
<ul>
<li>Roof and siding damage on 1980s–90s homes</li>
<li>Interior water losses from plumbing and water heaters</li>
<li>Townhome and condo coverage-split disputes</li>
<li>Tree damage near Lums Pond and wooded lots</li>
<li>Commercial roof claims along Route 40</li>
</ul>

